# cdncheck — CDN/WAF/cloud classification for an IP (ProjectDiscovery).
# Built from a pinned source tag; VERSION is our published image tag, REF the
# exact upstream ref. Bump REF to upgrade; the pipeline re-runs govulncheck +
# Trivy before publishing.
REPO=https://github.com/projectdiscovery/cdncheck
VERSION=v1.3.1
REF=v1.3.1
PKG=./cmd/cdncheck
BIN=cdncheck
# Go 1.26.8 — see planning#185 and the longer note in binaries/zgrab2/build.env.
# Pin the exact patch, not 1.26: a floating minor silently changes what the
# govulncheck gate verified.
GO_VERSION=1.26.8
# GO-2026-5320: goldmark XSS, reached via cdncheck's self-update callback
# (runner.GetUpdateCallback -> update.GetUpdateToolFromRepoCallback).
GO_PATCHES=github.com/yuin/goldmark@v1.7.17
#
# ─── Accepted govulncheck finding, with an expiry ───────────────────────────
#
# GO-2026-5932 (x/crypto/openpgp: unmaintained, unsafe by design) is reachable
# and has NO fixed version - the package is deprecated outright, so bumping
# x/crypto cannot clear it. It arrives via:
#
#   cdncheck/internal/runner -> projectdiscovery/utils/update
#     -> google/go-github/v30 -> golang.org/x/crypto/openpgp
#
# go-github v30 is from 2020; current go-github has no x/crypto dependency at
# all, so the upstream fix is a go-github bump in projectdiscovery/utils.
# Filed: https://github.com/projectdiscovery/utils/issues/784 (reported against
#   cdncheck, transferred by PD to utils — the repo where the fix belongs).
#
#   FIX MERGED 2026-09-21, NOT YET RELEASED.
#   https://github.com/projectdiscovery/utils/pull/785 bumps go-github
#   v30 -> v92 — exactly the change this needs, since current go-github has no
#   x/crypto dependency at all. Merged 2026-09-21T20:26Z as 8170b857f2c3b394.
#   That PR also raises utils to `go 1.26.0`; GO_VERSION is already there
#   (planning#185), so the toolchain does not stand in the way of adopting it.
#
#   WHAT TO CHECK NOW — the question has changed. It is no longer "is #785
#   open"; it is merged. It is "has utils tagged anything that CONTAINS
#   8170b857f2c3b394":
#
#     gh api repos/projectdiscovery/utils/tags --jq '.[0].name'
#     gh api repos/projectdiscovery/utils/compare/8170b857f2c3b394...<tag> --jq '.status'
#
#   "ahead" or "identical" => that tag carries the fix.  "behind" => it does not.
#
#   ⚠ A NEWER TAG IS NOT ENOUGH, and this is not hypothetical. v0.11.5 sorts
#   above v0.11.4 and is still WRONG: it was cut 2026-09-14, a week before the
#   merge, and compares `behind_by=4` against it. Checking the tag list alone
#   would have adopted a release that does not contain the fix and left the
#   advisory live while looking resolved. Compare against the commit, always.
#
#   When a tag does carry it: add github.com/projectdiscovery/utils@<tag> to
#   GO_PATCHES above and DELETE this acceptance. No need to wait for a cdncheck
#   release — GO_PATCHES overrides the transitive dep directly.
#
#   Deliberately NOT pinning the pseudo-version (utils@v0.11.6-0.2026...-
#   8170b857f2c3) to take it early: that adopts untagged upstream and a
#   62-major-version transitive jump on unreleased code, traded against an
#   acceptance that is argued, time-boxed, and has runway. Reviewed and
#   rejected 2026-09-22 — revisit only if the expiry gets close with no tag.
#
#   As of 2026-09-22: latest tag v0.11.5, does NOT contain the fix. Nothing to
#   pin yet.
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-16, accepted for 30 days on these grounds: there is no fixed
# version to move to; the advisory is a package-deprecation notice rather than
# an exploitable defect on our path; every trace is init-time registration
# inside the self-update machinery; and `-duc` means the updater never fetches
# or verifies a release, so no untrusted PGP material is ever parsed.
#
# The expiry is not a formality. On 2026-10-17 this build starts failing, and
# the question to answer then is whether projectdiscovery/utils has bumped
# go-github - not whether to extend the date again.
GOVULN_ALLOW=GO-2026-5932:2026-10-16
#
# ─── Two things about this tool that are not obvious ────────────────────────
#
# 1. THE DATASET IS COMPILED IN, NOT FETCHED AT RUNTIME.
#    cdncheck `//go:embed`s `sources_data.json` (~2.4 MB of CIDR/ASN ranges)
#    into the binary at build time. "Auto-updating" therefore means "updates
#    when you rebuild", NOT "refreshes itself in production". That makes this
#    repo the update mechanism: the weekly schedule in build.yml re-runs the
#    security gate against this pin, and bumping REF is how the ranges move.
#    A long-lived deployment pinned to an old digest serves old ranges - so
#    treat a stale cdncheck pin the way you'd treat a stale dataset.
#
# 2. IT PHONES HOME ON EVERY RUN UNLESS YOU STOP IT.
#    Unless `-duc` / `-disable-update-check` is passed, cdncheck calls
#    ProjectDiscovery's version endpoint on every invocation
#    (internal/runner/options.go: `if !opts.DisableUpdateCheck`). Verified
#    with `--network none`: the check fails soft, so classification still
#    works without it - but every consumer MUST still pass `-duc`. An
#    outbound call per scan is latency we don't need, and a security product
#    should not phone home by default.
#
# ─── Why scratch is still the right base here ───────────────────────────────
#
# Upstream's own Dockerfile uses alpine + ca-certificates + bind-tools, so it
# is worth saying why we don't. Our use is IP -> {is_cdn, is_waf, is_cloud,
# provider} resolved purely from the embedded dataset: no DNS, no TLS, no
# network at all once `-duc` is set.
#
# Do NOT treat the absent CA bundle as a second line of defence against the
# phone-home. It is not one. projectdiscovery/utils/update builds its version
# check client with `InsecureSkipVerify: true` (update.go init()), so that call
# validates no certificate, needs no root store, and succeeds from scratch.
# Verified: with a network and without `-duc`, this image still logs
# "Current cdncheck version v1.3.1 (latest)"; only `--network none` suppresses
# it. `-duc` is the ONLY thing that stops the outbound call.
#
# That same flag means the version check is unauthenticated in the TLS sense:
# encrypted against a passive observer, but an on-path attacker can impersonate
# api.pdtm.sh with any certificate. The realistic abuse is suppression - being
# told you are on the latest version when you are not. It does not extend to
# the binary download, which uses a different client and validates release
# checksums. Another reason every consumer passes `-duc`.
#
# The trade-off, recorded so it isn't rediscovered the hard way: cdncheck's
# CNAME-based detection needs DNS, and its ipinfo ASN path needs TLS. Neither
# works in scratch. If a future consumer wants either, that needs a base with
# a CA bundle and a resolver - a deliberate change, not a silent one.
